Technological determinism is a term that encompasses a variety of practical ideas, ranging from technological advancement or necessity to the precise significance that human destiny is determined by the inner logic related to the laws of science and their manifestation in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism have two common ideas: that the very development of technology goes in the direction, fundamentally surpassing cultural or political influence, and that the technology in turn exerts an "effect" on society, which is inherent, and not dependent on social conditions.
For example, someone can say that when you create a car, the roads will necessarily be parked.
However, the national road network is not economically viable if only a handful of cars use it, resulting in the development of new production methods aimed at reducing the cost of automotive ownership.
As a result of the significant prevalence of cars on the roads, the number of accidents increases, which contributes to the invention of new health care methods to treat bodily injuries.
